---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc946.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — the Investment Company Criteria" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Fundamental Principle — Fair Value for ALL Investments, No Consolidation" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The NAV Practical Expedient" },
        { id: "s5", label: "THE KEY — Broadly Converged With IFRS 10's Investment Entity Exception" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example — Measuring a Majority-Owned Investee at Fair Value" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Applying the NAV Practical Expedient" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s10", label: "ASC 946 vs IFRS 10 Investment Entity — Comparison" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A deliberate, fundamental exception to normal consolidation principles</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">A genuine INVESTMENT COMPANY — a fund pooling investor capital purely for INVESTMENT RETURNS — presents a unique measurement question: if it happens to own a CONTROLLING interest in an investee (say, 60% of a portfolio company), should it CONSOLIDATE that investee under normal ASC 810 principles? ASC 946 answers NO — investment companies measure ALL their investments, INCLUDING controlling interests, at FAIR VALUE through earnings, with NO consolidation — a deliberate, fundamental departure from the general consolidation framework, reflecting that fair value information is far more DECISION-USEFUL to fund investors than a consolidated operating-company presentation would be.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — the Investment Company Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Multiple criteria establishing genuine investment company status</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>Criterion</span><span>Description</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>1</span><span>Obtains funds from ONE OR MORE investors, providing professional INVESTMENT MANAGEMENT services</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>2</span><span>Its BUSINESS PURPOSE is investing SOLELY for returns — capital appreciation, investment income, or both</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>3</span><span>Investors hold UNIT ownership interests, with NO ability to exercise operational control over day-to-day investee management</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>4</span><span>Involves a POOLING of investor funds, so the entity's investors are not exposed to the risks of a single, wholly-owned operating business alone</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>5</span><span>The entity's investment plans provide for reasonably defined EXIT strategies from its investments (e.g. sale, IPO)</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Fundamental Principle — Fair Value for ALL Investments, No Consolidation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Even a controlling interest is measured at fair value, not consolidated</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">A qualifying investment company measures ALL its investments — regardless of the size of its ownership stake, INCLUDING investments where it holds a CONTROLLING financial interest — at <strong>FAIR VALUE</strong>, with changes recognised through EARNINGS. This is a GENUINE, deliberate SCOPE EXCEPTION carved out of ASC 810's general consolidation framework specifically for qualifying investment companies — recognising that consolidating a portfolio company's individual assets/liabilities line-by-line would OBSCURE, rather than clarify, the fund's true investment performance for its investors.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The NAV Practical Expedient</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Using net asset value as a practical fair value proxy</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">For investments in certain OTHER investment funds (e.g. a fund-of-funds structure) that don't have a readily determinable fair value, ASC 820 provides a PRACTICAL EXPEDIENT allowing the investor to use the investee fund's reported NET ASSET VALUE (NAV) per share as a PROXY for fair value, rather than requiring an independent fair value estimate — a pragmatic simplification given that the underlying fund itself already computes and reports NAV as part of its own normal operations.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>THE KEY — Broadly Converged With IFRS 10's Investment Entity Exception</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Both frameworks independently arrived at a similar exception, following the 2008 financial crisis</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">IFRS 10 contains its OWN specific "INVESTMENT ENTITY" exception (added via 2012 amendments, effective 2014) that SIMILARLY requires qualifying investment entities to measure subsidiaries at FAIR VALUE THROUGH PROFIT OR LOSS, rather than CONSOLIDATING them — a genuinely CONVERGED principle, arrived at through SEPARATE standard-setting processes on each side, both responding to similar post-2008-financial-crisis discussions about the importance of fair value transparency for fund investors. The specific ELIGIBILITY CRITERIA differ somewhat in wording and emphasis between ASC 946 and IFRS 10's investment entity definition, but the CORE outcome — fair value measurement of investments INCLUDING controlling interests, in lieu of consolidation — is SHARED by both frameworks.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — Measuring a Majority-Owned Investee at Fair Value</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">No consolidation, despite a controlling stake</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A private equity fund's 70% controlling stake</div><div class="wk-body">A qualifying investment company (a private equity fund) holds a 70% controlling equity stake in a portfolio manufacturing company, with an estimated fair value of $85,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Despite holding a CONTROLLING (70%) interest — which would normally trigger CONSOLIDATION under ASC 810 — the fund instead recognises a SINGLE line-item investment of <strong>$85,000,000</strong>, measured at FAIR VALUE through earnings, with changes in fair value recognised each period — rather than consolidating the portfolio company's individual assets, liabilities, revenues, and expenses line-by-line.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Applying the NAV Practical Expedient</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Using reported NAV as a fair value proxy</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: An investment in a hedge fund-of-funds</div><div class="wk-body">A fund-of-funds vehicle holds an interest in an underlying hedge fund that reports a NAV per share of $142.50, with no readily determinable fair value otherwise available for this illiquid interest.</div><div class="wk-answer">The investor USES the $142.50 reported NAV per share as a PRACTICAL EXPEDIENT proxy for fair value, multiplying by the number of shares/units held — rather than attempting an independent valuation of the underlying hedge fund's own investment portfolio.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 946 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Schedule of investments, showing individual holdings and their fair values</span><span>Basis for determining the entity qualifies as an investment company</span><span>Use of the NAV practical expedient and the nature of underlying investee funds</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether an entity meets all the qualifying investment company criteria</span><span>Whether the NAV practical expedient is appropriately applied to a given investee fund interest</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of illiquid or privately-held portfolio investments</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>ASC 946 vs IFRS 10 Investment Entity — Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 946 vs IFRS 10's investment entity exception</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 946)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 10)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Consolidation exception for controlling interests</span><span>YES — measured at fair value instead</span><span>YES — IFRS 10's investment entity exception, converged in principle</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Specific eligibility criteria wording</span><span>Five-criteria ASC 946 framework</span><span>IFRS 10's own investment entity definition — similar substance, different specific wording/emphasis</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>NAV practical expedient</span><span>Available under ASC 820</span><span>IFRS 13 has a broadly similar NAV practical expedient</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">This is one of the more GENUINELY converged industry Topics in the library — both frameworks independently concluded that fair value, not consolidation, best serves fund investors, even though the two exceptions were developed through separate standard-setting tracks.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 946 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Investment (including controlling interests)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Measured at fair value through earnings, not consolidated</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>NAV practical expedient investments</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Measured using reported NAV as a fair value proxy</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 810</span><span>Supplies the general consolidation framework that ASC 946 provides a scope exception from</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 820</span><span>Supplies the fair value measurement framework and NAV practical expedient</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Investment company</div><div class="glossary-def">An entity meeting specific criteria — pooling investor funds solely for investment returns with defined exit strategies — that measures all investments at fair value rather than consolidating controlling interests.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">NAV practical expedient</div><div class="glossary-def">A permitted simplification allowing an investor to use an investee fund's reported net asset value per share as a proxy for fair value.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc946 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

